{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We have a problem: count binary words of length 12 that do not contain any forbidden substring \"111\" or \"000\". So we need number of binary strings of length 12 avoiding runs of 3 consecutive identical bits. This is a combinatorial counting problem. We can use recurrence or automaton. Let's compute.\n\nWe need to count sequences of length n over alphabet {0,1} with no run of 3 identical bits. Equivalent to sequences where max run length <=2. Count for n=12.\n\nWe can use DP with states representing last two bits maybe. Let's define DP[n][last_bit][run_length] where run_length is 1 or 2. But we can also use recurrence: Let a_n be number of valid strings of length n ending with a run of length 1 (i.e., last bit different from previous), and b_n be number ending with run length 2 (i.e., last two bits same, but previous bit different). Then recurrence:\n\nFor n=1: a_1 = 2 (strings \"0\",\"1\" both run length 1). b_1 = 0.\n\nFor n>1: To get a_n: we need last bit different from previous, so we can append opposite bit to any string of length n-1 regardless of its run length. So a_n = a_{n-1} + b_{n-1}.\n\nTo get b_n: we need last two bits same, so we must append same bit as previous, but previous run length must be 1 (since if previous run length 2, appending same would make run length 3 forbidden). So b_n = a_{n-1}.\n\nThus recurrence: a_n = a_{n-1} + b_{n-1} = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}. And b_n = a_{n-1}.\n\nWe can compute.\n\nInitialize: a_1=2, b_1=0.\n\nn=2: a_2 = a_1 + b_1 = 2+0=2. b_2 = a_1 = 2.\n\nn=3: a_3 = a_2 + b_2 = 2+2=4. b_3 = a_2 = 2.\n\nn=4: a_4 = a_3 + b_3 = 4+2=6. b_4 = a_3 = 4.\n\nn=5: a_5 = a_4 + b_4 = 6+4=10. b_5 = a_4 = 6.\n\nn=6: a_6 = a_5 + b_5 = 10+6=16. b_6 = a_5 = 10.\n\nn=7: a_7 = a_6 + b_6 = 16+10=26. b_7 = a_6 = 16.\n\nn=8: a_8 = a_7 + b_7 = 26+16=42. b_8 = a_7 = 26.\n\nn=9: a_9 = a_8 + b_8 = 42+26=68. b_9 = a_8 = 42.\n\nn=10: a_10 = a_9 +